        <ArticleTitle>Interleukin-6 signaling and macrophage polarization in chronic inflammation.</ArticleTitle>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Chronic inflammation is driven in part by cytokine signaling that reprograms tissue-resident macrophages.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We profiled macrophage phenotypes under sustained IL-6 exposure using single-cell RNA sequencing in a synthetic dataset.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Sustained IL-6 shifted macrophages toward an M2-like state with elevated STAT3 activity.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">IL-6/STAT3 signaling is a candidate axis for modulating macrophage polarization in chronic inflammatory disease.</AbstractText>
