        <ArticleTitle>Amyloid-beta clearance via the glymphatic system during sleep.</ArticleTitle>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The glymphatic system is hypothesized to clear metabolic waste, including amyloid-beta, from the brain during sleep.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In a synthetic murine model, slow-wave sleep increased simulated amyloid-beta clearance by 45% relative to wakefulness, mediated by AQP4 water channels.</AbstractText>
          <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These fabricated results support a link between sleep architecture and amyloid clearance relevant to neurodegeneration.</AbstractText>
